1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,722
They thought they had time.

2
00:00:03,722 --> 00:00:07,443
It is the morning of the 24th of August.

3
00:00:07,443 --> 00:00:11,165
The city of Pompeii is awake,
and ordinary.

4
00:00:11,165 --> 00:00:14,886
Bread is baking.

5
00:00:14,886 --> 00:00:18,608
The markets are opening.

6
00:00:18,608 --> 00:00:22,330
A dog sleeps in a doorway in
the early heat.

7
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:26,051
Above the town, the mountain they call Vesuvius sits quiet, the way it
has sat quiet for as long as anyone alive can remember.

8
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:29,773
No one in the city below knows that
it is a volcano.

9
00:00:29,773 --> 00:00:33,494
There is no word, yet, for what is
about to happen to them.

10
00:00:33,494 --> 00:00:37,216
A little after noon, the mountain opens.

11
00:00:37,216 --> 00:00:40,938
A column of ash and rock climbs into the sky —
higher than the eye can follow,

12
00:00:40,938 --> 00:00:44,659
twelve miles, then more — and at
the top it spreads,

13
00:00:44,659 --> 00:00:48,381
and turns the afternoon to night.

14
00:00:48,381 --> 00:00:52,102
And here is the strange part. The part that is easy to forget, two thousand
years later, when we look at the bodies frozen in the ash.

15
00:00:52,102 --> 00:00:55,824
They were warned.

16
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:59,546
They had hours. A whole day, for
some of them.

17
00:00:59,546 --> 00:01:03,267
Time to gather what mattered, to reach the gate,
to walk down to the sea and away.

18
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:06,989
Some did. The ones who ran early
— they lived.

19
00:01:06,989 --> 00:01:10,710
But many stayed.

20
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:14,432
They closed their shutters against the falling ash
and they waited for it to pass,

21
00:01:14,432 --> 00:01:18,154
the way bad weather passes.

22
00:01:18,154 --> 00:01:21,875
They did not know that this was
not weather.

23
00:01:21,875 --> 00:01:25,597
They did not know that the air itself
would come for them.

24
00:01:25,597 --> 00:01:29,318
This is the story of the
people who waited.

25
00:01:29,318 --> 00:01:33,040
And of one family, down at the water's edge, who
believed — right until the end —

26
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:36,762
that the sea would carry them home.

27
00:01:36,762 --> 00:01:40,483
[Here's what you need to understand.]

28
00:01:40,483 --> 00:01:44,205
To understand why they stayed, you have to
understand what they were seeing.

29
00:01:44,205 --> 00:01:47,926
Because it did not look like
death. Not at first.

30
00:01:47,926 --> 00:01:51,648
For the first few hours, the danger
was almost gentle.

31
00:01:51,648 --> 00:01:55,370
Ash fell like grey snow.

32
00:01:55,370 --> 00:01:59,091
Small stones of pumice — light, porous, warm
— rattled against the roofs.

33
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:02,813
A nuisance. A strange afternoon.

34
00:02:02,813 --> 00:02:06,534
People swept their doorsteps.

35
00:02:06,534 --> 00:02:10,256
They stood in the streets and pointed at the column rising over the
mountain, the way we would film a storm rolling in.

36
00:02:10,256 --> 00:02:13,977
The ones who left in those first hours made a
quiet, unremarkable decision.

37
00:02:13,977 --> 00:02:17,699
They gathered a few things.

38
00:02:17,699 --> 00:02:21,421
They took the children's hands.

39
00:02:21,421 --> 00:02:25,142
And they walked out through the city gates while the roads were
still open, while the sky to the south was still clear.

40
00:02:25,142 --> 00:02:28,864
History does not remember them. They simply lived, and went on, and told
their grandchildren about the day the mountain woke up.

41
00:02:28,864 --> 00:02:32,585
It is the others we remember.

42
00:02:32,585 --> 00:02:36,307
Because the pumice kept falling.
Hour after hour.

43
00:02:36,307 --> 00:02:40,029
And it began to pile — on the streets,
against the doors, on the roofs.

44
00:02:40,029 --> 00:02:43,750
A foot of it. Then more.

45
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:47,472
And now leaving was no longer a
simple walk to the gate.

46
00:02:47,472 --> 00:02:51,193
Now it meant wading through deepening stone, in the dark,
with ash filling your eyes and your mouth,

47
00:02:51,193 --> 00:02:54,915
not knowing if the road still led
anywhere at all.

48
00:02:54,915 --> 00:02:58,637
So a terrible kind of logic took hold.

49
00:02:58,637 --> 00:03:02,358
If you had already waited this long — if the worst
so far had only been falling stone —

50
00:03:02,358 --> 00:03:06,080
then surely the safest thing was to stay inside. To shelter.
To wait for morning, and for it to be over.

51
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,801
They were wrong. But you can see how a reasonable person, in that
moment, with what they knew, would choose to stay.

52
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:13,523
And then, in the darkness before dawn, the mountain did
the thing they could not have imagined.

53
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:17,245
The column that had been climbing into the sky
for twelve hours collapsed.

54
00:03:17,245 --> 00:03:20,966
And what came down was not ash,
and not stone.

55
00:03:20,966 --> 00:03:24,688
It was a wave of superheated gas and rock, moving down the slope of the mountain faster
than a person can run — faster, almost, than a person can comprehend.

56
00:03:24,688 --> 00:03:28,409
Hundreds of degrees. Silent until
the last seconds.

57
00:03:28,409 --> 00:03:32,131
It did not bury them slowly. It
reached them in moments.

58
00:03:32,131 --> 00:03:35,853
This is what the ash preserved. Not the bodies,
exactly — those were gone long ago.

59
00:03:35,853 --> 00:03:39,574
But the spaces where the bodies had been. The
hollows in the hardened ash.

60
00:03:39,574 --> 00:03:43,296
And when archaeologists, centuries later, filled those hollows with plaster, the
people of Pompeii came back — in the exact posture of their last moment.

61
00:03:43,296 --> 00:03:47,017
A man shielding his face.

62
00:03:47,017 --> 00:03:50,739
A dog, still chained, twisted
against its collar.

63
00:03:50,739 --> 00:03:54,461
A pair of figures lying together, one arm
thrown over the other.

64
00:03:54,461 --> 00:03:58,182
They are not statues. They are the shape of a real person's
final second, held for two thousand years.

65
00:03:58,182 --> 00:04:01,904
And down at the shore — at the harbour town of
Herculaneum, in the boat chambers along the water —

66
00:04:01,904 --> 00:04:05,625
they found them in groups.

67
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:09,347
Families. Dozens of them, gathered in the stone
arches that opened onto the sea.

68
00:04:09,347 --> 00:04:13,069
They had not stayed in their homes. They
had done the sensible thing.

69
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:16,790
They had run toward the water, toward escape, toward
the boats that surely would come.

70
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:20,512
A woman with her jewellery still on.

71
00:04:20,512 --> 00:04:24,233
A soldier.

72
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:27,955
A mother holding an infant
against her chest.

73
00:04:27,955 --> 00:04:31,677
They had reached the shore. They were
waiting for rescue.

74
00:04:31,677 --> 00:04:35,398
And the historians believe a rescue was even
attempted — that ships were sent,

75
00:04:35,398 --> 00:04:39,120
and turned back by the sea and the falling debris, unable to reach
the people standing on the beach watching for them.

76
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:42,841
So they waited in the boat chambers, out of the
falling stone, looking out at the water.

77
00:04:42,841 --> 00:04:46,563
And the wave from the mountain
reached them there.

78
00:04:46,563 --> 00:04:50,285
They died in the one place they were
certain they were safe.

79
00:04:50,285 --> 00:04:54,006
Together. Facing the sea.

80
00:04:54,006 --> 00:04:57,728
We know all of this — the morning, the column, the hours of
falling stone, the families at the shore —

81
00:04:57,728 --> 00:05:01,449
because of the thing that killed them.

82
00:05:01,449 --> 00:05:05,171
The ash that took their lives is the same ash that
sealed their city away from time.

83
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:08,893
No looters. No rebuilding. No two thousand
years of weather wearing it down.

84
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:12,614
Just a moment, covered, and kept.

85
00:05:12,614 --> 00:05:16,336
Pompeii is not a story about a volcano.

86
00:05:16,336 --> 00:05:20,057
We have had many volcanoes.

87
00:05:20,057 --> 00:05:23,779
It is a story about people who
were given time,

88
00:05:23,779 --> 00:05:27,501
and used it the way any of us might have —

89
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:31,222
by waiting, by hoping, by trusting that
tomorrow would look like yesterday.

90
00:05:31,222 --> 00:05:34,944
They thought they had time.

91
00:05:34,944 --> 00:05:38,665
The terrible truth is that they did.

92
00:05:38,665 --> 00:05:42,387
They just did not know what it was for.
